{
  "case_id": "2017-16-299",
  "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "National Association of Manufacturers",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Clean Water Act provides for\njudicial review in the courts of appeals of\nseven categories of action by the EPA\nAdministrator, and those are defined narrowly\nand precisely in Section 1369(b)(1) of the Act.\nHad Congress meant for courts of\nappeals to review all national or definitional\nrules, it would have said so, as it did in the\nClean Air Act, instead of listing a handful of\nparticular EPA actions down to the statutory\nsubsection.\nOur textual approach to subsections\n(b)(1) and (E) -- (b)(1)(E) and (F) results in\na comparatively clear jurisdictional rule that"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What would happen\nif two questions were presented? The first\ninvolves whether the water in question fits\nwithin \"waters of the United States\"; that's a\npreliminary question. And then there's a\nchallenge to a grant -- a grant or denial of a\npermit.\nIf you had those two combined, where\ndo they go?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "National Association of Manufacturers",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "one of the consequences that your\nopponent points out is that if you're correct\nand these actions are brought in the district\ncourt, each of the district courts will have to\nreview the entire administrative record, and\npresumably, you could have dozens of the\ndistrict courts engaged in that same activity,\nand then it would have to be done all over\nagain when you get to the court of appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Let me ask you\nsomething. Is -- I don't think there's any res\njudicata against the government, or is there?\nWould a -- would a -- or collateral estoppel,\nit wouldn't be res judicata, could there be\ncollateral estoppel?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ohio, et al.",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nothing in subsection or, excuse\nme, nothing in Section 1311 directs them to do\nthat. That would be the agencies acting under\ntheir general rule-making authority, which\nwould be in Section 1361(a). That's the\ncatch-all. It says, the agencies, you can\nissue rules to implement the Act. That's exactly what the WOTUS rule is designed to\naccomplish. It's under that authority."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: But do you think that the EPA has the power to issue the type of rule that Justice Kagan described?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Murphy",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MURPHY: They very may not have the power. If the -- if the power exists, however, I do not think it would be a power under 1311. I think it would -- the power would flow from 1361, which is the general authority to implement the Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Do you -- do you think general -- I mean, does your interpretation depend very much on a specific understanding of the word \"under\"? In other words, you are reading this to say something like under the specific authority of Section 1311, 1312. But \"under\" is a kind of nebulous word. It doesn't say under the specific authority here. It just says \"under.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All right. So just realistically, is it possible this case would be mooted this term or is this process one that innately will take longer than this term?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-16-299",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "3e86501da6dfa2c9530ed2d4b85080b74b40f0b7aa010ac7b85d55cb0fe77bbb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3e86501da6df-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could I ask you about clause (F)? What is your textual argument relating to that?"
    },
